<p>When a pulse remains hard after a long cooking time, splits completely or sheds its skin, we often blame the clock. In reality, final texture starts being determined long before the pan is heated. <strong>Age, storage history, water composition, soaking and the ingredients added to the pot</strong> all influence how quickly water penetrates and how cell walls soften.</p>
<p>Understanding those mechanisms is more useful than rigid rules such as “chickpeas always take X minutes” or “salt must never be added early”. Pulses are agricultural products: different harvests, varieties and domestic water supplies can behave differently.</p>
<h2>Why a dried pulse softens during cooking</h2>
<p>During soaking and cooking, the seed absorbs water. Heat changes starches, proteins and cell-wall structures, taking the texture from dry and brittle to tender and creamy. The aim is not merely to force water inside but to let hydration and heat progress reasonably evenly.</p>
<p>If the exterior weakens too quickly before the centre is tender, skins split and grains burst. If hydration or cell-wall softening is slowed, the centre can remain firm even after prolonged cooking.</p>
<h2>The age of the dried pulse matters</h2>
<p>Dried pulses keep for a long time, but they do not remain identical forever. With age and certain storage conditions they can develop the phenomenon known as <strong>hard-to-cook</strong>: the seed takes much longer to soften and, in severe cases, never develops a pleasant texture despite extended cooking.</p>
<p>Good stock rotation therefore matters. Buy from producers and shops with sensible turnover, keep pulses cool and dry, and use older lots first. A bag forgotten for years at the back of a cupboard is not a fair test of the variety.</p>
<h2>Hard water can slow softening</h2>
<p>Water containing higher levels of calcium and magnesium can strengthen parts of the cell-wall structure and slow softening. This is one reason the same bean can behave differently in two cities.</p>
<p>If your pulses repeatedly stay hard despite correct soaking and long cooking, try one batch with softer or lower-mineral water. The purpose is not to create another kitchen ritual; it is to isolate a variable and see whether local water is part of the problem.</p>
<h2>Soaking does not matter equally for every pulse</h2>
<p>Chickpeas and many dried beans usually benefit from soaking because the seed hydrates before cooking begins. Lentils, depending on variety and size, often need no soak or a much shorter one.</p>
<p>Extremely long soaking is not automatically better, particularly in a warm room. Use clean water and appropriate conditions. In hot weather, soaking under refrigeration is a sensible option.</p>
<h2>Why acidic ingredients can keep pulses firm</h2>
<p>Tomato, wine, vinegar, citrus and other acidic ingredients can slow the softening of some pulses. If your goal is a very tender result, adding them once the seed is already well advanced in cooking often works better.</p>
<p>This does not mean every recipe containing tomato from the start will fail. But when the classic problem is “it has been cooking for two hours and is still hard”, the timing of acidic ingredients is one of the first things worth checking.</p>
<h2>Salt: one of the most persistent myths</h2>
<p>For years, home cooks have been told that early salting inevitably makes pulses tough. Culinary reality is more nuanced. Salt can influence skins, seasoning and hydration, but it is not a simple switch that condemns a pot of beans.</p>
<p>Some cooks salt soaking or cooking water early to produce well-seasoned seeds with more stable skins; others adjust later. Do not let the salt myth distract from factors that are often much more important, such as very old pulses, hard water and strong acidity.</p>
<h2>Why beans split or lose their skins</h2>
<p>A violent rolling boil makes hydrated beans collide with each other and the pan. Once skins are delicate, that movement encourages breakage. Constant vigorous stirring can do the same.</p>
<p>For beans you want to serve whole, a steady, moderate cook after the appropriate temperature is reached is generally gentler. Moving the pan carefully is less aggressive than repeatedly stirring with a spoon. In a stew where some breakdown is desirable to thicken the broth, a little disintegration may actually be useful.</p>
<h2>Chickpeas hard in the centre: what to check</h2>
<p>If a chickpea feels soft outside but keeps a hard core, review four variables: age, soaking time, water hardness and acidic ingredients. More cooking can correct a simple lack of time, but it cannot always compensate for very old seed or unfavourable water.</p>
<p>Keep enough cooking liquid in the pot as well. A pan that nearly dries out no longer transfers heat in the same way and increases the chance of uneven cooking.</p>
<h2>Baking soda can speed softening, but it is not neutral</h2>
<p>A more alkaline environment can accelerate softening, which is why some methods use a small amount of baking soda, especially with hard water or recipes where a very creamy texture is wanted. Too much, however, can turn tender into mushy, change flavour and encourage skins to detach.</p>
<p>It is not an essential ingredient. Before using it, correct soaking, water, pulse age and the timing of acidic ingredients.</p>
<h2>A pressure cooker changes time, not the fundamentals</h2>
<p>Pressure raises cooking temperature and can dramatically reduce time, but an old bean is still old and a strongly acidic environment still affects softening. A pressure cooker is a tool, not a way to erase the characteristics of the ingredient.</p>
<p>Times vary greatly between cooker models, varieties and quantities, so use appliance and producer instructions as a starting point and adjust from experience with that specific batch.</p>
<h2>Diagnose the problem instead of guessing</h2>
<p>If one batch goes wrong, change one variable next time. Use the same pulses with different water, or keep the water and add tomato later, or keep the method and alter soaking. Changing five variables at once teaches you nothing about the cause.</p>
<p>That approach turns a failed recipe into useful information. Within a few cooks you can learn how a particular variety behaves in your kitchen.</p>
<h2>Not every pulse should end with the same texture</h2>
<p>Lentils for a salad should remain firm. Stew beans may be best with a creamy centre and barely noticeable skin. Chickpeas for hummus need to be much softer than chickpeas for a salad. “Never let them split” is therefore not a universal rule either.</p>
<p>Good cooking produces the texture the dish requires. Our <a href="/en/complete-guide-to-cooking-dried-pulses-soaking-cooking-and-common-mistakes/">complete guide to cooking dried pulses</a> covers the general process, while <a href="/en/chickpeas-lentils-beans-differences-and-cooking-uses/">chickpeas, lentils and beans: differences and uses</a> helps match each family to the right dish.</p>
<h2>An important food-safety note</h2>
<p>Some pulses, particularly certain beans, require complete cooking and should not be eaten raw or undercooked. Specific guidance varies by product and method, so follow package instructions and applicable official food-safety advice. A slow cooker should not automatically be treated as a substitute for any required cooking step for a pulse that needs one.</p>
